---
title: "Joining frames"
description: "The four join kinds and how key columns are matched."
---

Joining is how a query reaches data that lives in a second frame. Tarn's `join` is
lazy like everything else, and the planner is free to reorder it against the filters
around it.

# Join kinds {#sec-join-kinds}

Four kinds are supported, selected with `how=`:

| `how=` | keeps |
|--------|-------|
| `inner` (default) | rows with a match on both sides |
| `left` | every left row; unmatched right columns are null |
| `outer` | every row from both sides |
| `anti` | left rows with **no** match on the right |

`anti` is the one people forget exists and then reimplement with a `left` join and an
`is_null` filter. It is the same result and a slower plan.

## Matching keys {#sec-join-keys}

`on=` names a column present in both frames. When the two sides name the key
differently, give both:

```python
enriched = sales.join(regions, left_on="region_code", right_on="code")
```

A key column that is null never matches, including against another null — the same
three-valued rule that governs filtering. A frame whose key column contains nulls
therefore loses those rows in an inner join, silently.

## Overlapping column names {#sec-join-overlap}

When both sides carry a column of the same name and it is not the key, the right
side's copy is suffixed with `_right`. Rename before joining if that suffix would end
up in a published result:

```python
enriched = sales.join(regions.rename({"name": "region_name"}), on="region")
```
